r law enforcement, media, and social structures have created a distorted lens. It doesn't mean that Black residents are inherently more involved in drugs. In fact, national data consistently shows drug use is not racially distinct, and in many categories, white people use drugs at higher rates. For your car park situation, relying on this distorted lens is exactly the problem. If security acts on the basis of “that’s where the news says drug busts happen,” instead of on actual, observed, race-neutral suspicious behavior in your specific location, they'll be doing bad security and illegal discrimination. The goal is to stop drug dealing in your car park, not to replicate broader societal biases—and that means treating every resident based only on what they do, not who they are.
